<h1>Sense Data</h1><div id="pubinfo"><em>First published Mon Aug 2, 2021; substantive revision Thu Aug 13, 2026</em></div>

<div id="preamble">

<p>
&ldquo;Sense data&rdquo;, or &ldquo;sense datum&rdquo; in the
singular, is a technical term in philosophy that means &ldquo;what is
given to sense&rdquo;. Sense data putatively constitute what we, as
perceiving subjects, are directly aware of in perceptual experience,
prior to cognitive acts such as inferring, judging, or affirming that
such-and-such objects or properties are present. In vision, sense data
are typically described as exhibiting colors and shapes. For the other
senses, they would manifest sounds, tastes, odors, and tactile
qualities. Suppose that you are looking at a brown table with a white
coaster on it; your sense data would be a patch of brown corresponding
to the brown expanse in your field of view, along with a
roundish-shaped white patch. The table might in some places appear
white because of glare. Based on such data, you might affirm that a
brown thing and a white thing, or a table and a coaster, are present
before you.</p>

<p>
Starting in the early twentieth century, the notion of sense data
played a central role in discussions of knowledge and of the ontology
of perceptual experience. In epistemology, sense data were to provide
the basis for all sensory knowledge. They were well suited for this
role, since they were considered to be epistemically privileged: many
authors held that one could not be wrong about one&rsquo;s sense data.
They manifested sensory properties in a way that made them easily
known as the &ldquo;given&rdquo; in experience.</p>

<p>
Another reason for positing sense data was provided by differences
that were noticed&mdash;or said to be noticed&mdash;between
characteristics of sensory experience and the presumed properties of
objects. From some viewing positions, a rectangular table may appear
trapezoidal and (as mentioned) parts of its brown surface may appear
as white. To account for these appearances and their difference from
the actual properties of the table, philosophers introduced sense data
as the bearers of properties such as being trapezoidal or being white.
Sense data enter into an account of how there might be a trapezoid
involved in the appearance of the table: perceivers directly
experience an actually trapezoidal sense datum.</p>

<p>
Concerning what sense data are (their ontology), there has been a
change of consensus over time. The early advocates of sense data
(explicitly so-called) mainly considered them to be mind-independent,
that is, to exist apart from the mind, perhaps as a special kind of
thing, neither mental nor physical. In the latter part of the
twentieth century, sense data came to be viewed most often as
mind-dependent, as mental objects or contents that we are directly
aware of in perceptual experience. They were thought to depend on the
mind for their existence.</p>

<p>
Sense data dominated discussion of perception in the first half of the
twentieth century. The notion has largely fallen out of favor and is
now often cited as a position to avoid. But it also has present-day
advocates.</p>

<p>
This entry first examines the classical meaning of the term
&ldquo;sense data&rdquo; and the concept of a sensory given. It then
pursues the history of these notions, from the early modern period to
the mid twentieth century, before turning to major critical responses
that led to a decline in the popularity of sense data. Finally, it
considers recent arguments in favor of sense data, as mental items, or
as portions of the physical world.</p>
</div> 

