<h2 id="TermConc">1. The Classical Notion of Sense Data: Term and Concept</h2>

<p>
Consider your present visual experience. Suppose that you are seated
at a table in a garden. You are looking at objects on the table and at
the vivid colors of flowers nearby. Now focus on a particular static
instance of your looking: the moment in which you see a white coaster
on a brown table with a cut daisy lying just beyond the coaster. The
sense data for this scene would be the particular shapes and colors as
immediately experienced, e.g., a white patch as seen in front of you.
You are looking at the coaster from an angle of about forty-five
degrees. At this angle, many observers report the momentary visual
experience of an elliptical white shape. (Try to experience this for
yourself by observing a round coaster on a table at the suggested
angle. As we will discover, not everyone agrees that the patch looks
elliptical; some find a circle at a slant.) The rectangular table top
is small enough that you can take it in at one glance: at this angle,
many describe their momentary visual experience as containing a
trapezoidal brown expanse, with the larger base of the trapezoid
toward them. The daisy presents sense data of a specific yellow and
round shape, white petal-shapes, and green leaf- and stem-shapes.</p>

<p>
These descriptions of sense data focus on what is said to be given to
the perceiver in the most elemental sensory experience: in this case,
colors and shapes. The facts that we are seeing a table, a coaster,
and a daisy are not, in the usual sense-data theories, part of what is
given. Typically, visual sense data are just shapes and colors; thus,
if the daisy were partly obscured by a mug that is placed on the
coaster, there would be two green shapes among the sense data, on
either side of the mug-shape, with no notion that the one belongs with
the other. Viewers typically are, of course, aware that specific
objects are present. In that case, the theory says that they have gone
beyond their sense data to perceive objects or things of various kinds
(table, coaster, flower), as opposed to the bare shape and color of
each sense datum.</p>

<p>
The term &ldquo;sense data&rdquo; was made prominent in philosophy
during the early decades of the twentieth century by G. E. Moore and
Bertrand Russell, followed by intense elaboration and modification of
the concept by C. D.
 Broad,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-1" id="ref-1">1</a>]</sup>
 H. H. Price, and A. J. Ayer, among others. Although the promoters of
sense data disagreed in various ways, they mainly agreed on the
following points:</p>

<ol type="1">

<li id="cn1">In sense perception, we are directly and immediately
aware of a sense datum.</li>

<li id="cn2">This awareness occurs by a relation of direct mental
acquaintance with a datum.</li>

<li id="cn3">Sense data have the properties that they appear to
have.</li>

<li id="cn4">These properties are determinate; in vision, we
experience determinate shapes, sizes, and colors.</li>

<li id="cn5">Our awareness of such properties of sense data does not
involve the affirmation or conception of any object beyond the
datum.</li>

<li id="cn6">These properties are known to us with certainty (and
perhaps infallibly).</li>

<li id="cn7">Sense data are private; a datum is apprehended by only
one person.</li>

<li id="cn8">Sense data are distinct from the act of sensing, or the
act by which we are aware of them.</li>
</ol>

<p>
This list constitutes a basic analysis of the Classical Notion of
sense data.
 <a href="#cn1">Item (1)</a>
 says that sense data are not perceived by the mediation of anything
else. This was often put by saying that sense data are the immediate
objects of acts of sensing.
 <a href="#cn2">Item (2)</a>
 states that our mental relation to sense data is one of direct
acquaintance.
 <a href="#cn3">Item (3)</a>
 says that if we immediately sense a brown patch of a certain shape,
we are acquainted with an item that has the phenomenal brownness and
the shape as present in our experience. That is, however color may be
considered in physical science (as a microphysical property of object
surfaces, for instance), the sense datum possesses the phenomenally
present color property.
 <a href="#cn4">Item (4)</a>
 indicates that such properties are experienced as determinate; for
example, we experience a specific shade of brown.
 <a href="#cn5">Item (5)</a>
 distinguishes the awareness of sensory qualities such as color and
shape from the judgment or affirmation that a material object (or
other entity) might (or might not) underlie the sense datum. It allows
the properties found in sense data may differ from the properties
found in their causes, as a trapezoid differs from a rectangle.
 <a href="#cn6">Item (6)</a>
 claims that each perceiver is certain about, and, in a stronger
version, cannot err about, there being an instance of brown color of a
certain shape that is experienced. This indicates that sense data can
be epistemically basic.
 <a href="#cn7">Item (7)</a>
 holds that sense data are private, or perhaps that they inhabit a
private space, so that sense data can be related to one another for
one perceiver, but the sense data of one perceiver are not in an
immediate relation to the sense data of another.
 <a href="#cn8">Item (8)</a>
 says that sense data are distinct from the mental acts by which we
perceive them; in the Classical Notion, this typically included that
they are not mind-dependent but are independent items.</p>

<p>
Sense data played the positive role of mediating between
object-properties and sensory experience. The table may be
rectangular, but it appears trapezoidal. What gives it that phenomenal
content? As we have seen, one answer is that sense data (whether
mental or non-mental) bear the property of being trapezoidal and
explain the experience. Another benefit is that sense data might
mitigate the difference between scientific accounts of perception and
common sense. It is tempting, commonsensically, to accept that our
phenomenal experience presents the properties of things as they are,
which, in vision, would include colors as we experience them. But
science tells us that color vision is mediated by wavelengths of light
that are not themselves colored in any common understanding of that
name. If phenomenally brown sense data are caused by neural activity
that is caused by the reception in the eye of physical light, these
sense data might provide an ontological home for phenomenal
colors.</p>

<p>
Drawbacks for sense data include doubts about needing a special
entity, a sense datum, to account for phenomenal experience. We shall
come across other explanations of phenomenal experience that
don&rsquo;t require new types of things. Another putative disadvantage
is that, as intermediaries, sense data facilitate a skeptical worry
about how an external world can be inferred from such data if our
sensory experience is limited to them. This is the problem of the veil
of perception&mdash;that phenomenally experienced sense data that are
caused by but distinct from objects form a curtain between perceivers
and those objects.</p>

<p>
Alongside benefits and drawbacks there have been numerous arguments
for sense data. The earliest of these is called the Argument from
Perceptual Variation, which used the table example to query what shape
we really see the table to have. Some theorists (Hume, as precursor;
Russell) drew on sense data to answer this question: the table that we
see appears trapezoidal, but the real table is rectangular, so the
table we see is not the real table. Second in popularity is the
Argument from Illusion, which starts by observing that illusory
perceptions (and also hallucinations), which don&rsquo;t match
reality, and veridical perceptions, which do, can be phenomenally
indistinguishable; there may be nothing about them that marks them as
illusory or veridical. In the illusory case, sense data are posited to
account for the phenomenal content that deviates from the external
object. But, since illusory and veridical cases are indistinguishable,
there must be sense data in the veridical case, as well. We will
consider these and additional arguments as we proceed.</p>

<p>
It may be helpful to mention several things that are not included in
the discussion of the Classical Notion. For instance, there is no
specification of whether sense data are two dimensional or contain
depth. Early theorists are typically read as asserting two-dimensional
 data;<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-2" id="ref-2">2</a>]</sup>
 the second wave, including Price (1932: 3, 218&ndash;220), explicitly
gave some of them depth. Also, there is no statement about whether
sense data persist beyond the momentary sensing of them, as there was
disagreement on this point. There is no assertion of their origin,
causal or otherwise. It is natural to think of sense data as caused by
material objects and as being related to those objects as appearance
to reality, a position expressed by some authors (e.g., Russell 1912:
ch. 1; Broad 1923: 237&ndash;240). It is also natural to regard sense
data as providing a basis for knowing the world as inferred from them.
But those conceptions are not included here in the agreed-upon
attributes of sense data in their early history.</p>

<p>
 <a href="#cn5">Item (5)</a>
 distinguishes the apprehension of a sense datum from that of a
material object or other entity. Item (5) does not say whether sense
data represent objects or their properties, independently of a further
act of judgment or affirmation; some classical theorists denied that
sense data are intrinsically representational. Further, sense data
were not tied to specific neurophysiological processes, even if it was
admitted by some authors that they are conditioned by
neurophysiological states (such as those that produce afterimages).
Finally, &ldquo;direct theorists&rdquo;, who hold that we perceive
objects or their properties directly, without intermediaries,
sometimes described the immediate apprehension of a portion of the
surface of an object as a sense datum. Present-day taxonomies of
philosophies of perception typically contrast direct realism (or
na&iuml;ve realism) with sense-data theory, an outlook that results in
part from the present tendency to treat sense data as mental (Crane
&amp; French 2015 [2021]). But in the classical period, there were
arguments to the effect that sense data are direct perceptions of a
part of the surface of a material object (Moore 1918&ndash;19:
23&ndash;24), a position that Price (1932: chs. 2&ndash;3) described
and sought to undermine.</p>

<p>
In the above statement of the Classical Notion, nothing explicit is
said about the metaphysical classification of sense data. They might
be events, substances, states of substances; they might be physical,
mental, or neither. As mentioned, originally the majority of authors
held that sense data are not mental. But some theorists advised that
the term &ldquo;sense data&rdquo; in its basic meaning should be
treated as neutral on ontology (e.g., Moore 1913&ndash;14; Price 1932:
18&ndash;20), even if further argument led to a view that they are not
mental. For the neutralists, accepting that sense data exist should be
compatible with various theories of perceptual ontology, such as
na&iuml;ve realism, representative realism, Berkeleyan idealism, or
Kantian transcendental idealism.</p>

<p>
Early theorists who considered sense data to be mind-independent
typically thought of them as persisting through time. Russell, in
early sense-data writings (1912: ch. 1), viewed such data as existing
apart from the mind as a special kind of thing (neither mental nor
physical), which was commonly designated as a <em>tertium quid</em> or
&ldquo;third thing&rdquo;, in addition to objects (such as a physical
table) and the perceiver&rsquo;s mental states. Such intermediary
third things might be epistemically given only in the act of sensing
them, but they would not depend for their existence on that act. This
led to the notion of unsensed sense data (e.g., mind-independent
patches of color), which were sometimes called
&ldquo;sensibilia&rdquo; to indicate that they could be sensed if
someone were at the right location, but that they existed in any case
(Russell 1914b: sec. 3). In the second half of the twentieth century,
when sense data came to be viewed mainly as mind-dependent, they were
considered to be mental objects or contents of which we are directly
aware. Accordingly, the white shape of the coaster-shaped patch would
be a property of the mental object that is experienced, which was
taken to mean that the mental state itself would have among its
properties being white and being round (Jackson 1977: chs.
4&ndash;5).</p>

<p>
The initial positing of sense data arose in discussions of sense
perception and its objects. In Oxford and Cambridge of the late
nineteenth century, it was common to discuss perception as part of the
&ldquo;problem of the external world&rdquo;, an epistemological
problem found in modern philosophy, from Descartes onward, concerning
whether we know an external world beyond our own minds and, if so, how
we know it. These discussions typically distinguished between
sensation and perception, in ways similar to the later distinction
between sense data and the perception of material objects. A standard
description of how vision occurs would say that sensations of shape
and color are first produced via the stimulation of the sensory
nerves, and that from such sensations the perceiver forms perceptions
of objects, perhaps through a process of judgment or through learned
associative connections among sensations. Some authors thought that
this process involved an inference to the existence and properties of
an external world. Let us turn, then, to the history of theories of
sensation and perception, and the rise of sense data.</p>

<h2 id="Hist">2. Historical</h2>

<p>
The philosophical practice of separating what is immediately given to
vision by way of sense impression from further psychological or
logical acts of perception stems from antiquity. Arguably, both
Aristotle and the Stoics made a distinction between sensation and
 perception.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-3" id="ref-3">3</a>]</sup>
 In the optical tradition (where &ldquo;optics&rdquo; covered the
complete science of vision, from physics and physiology to
psychology), the medieval Islamic philosopher Ibn al-Haytham (ca.
1030) distinguished what is found in &ldquo;pure sensation&rdquo;, the
immediate result of stimulating the sense organs, from what depends on
recognition, judgment, and reason (<em>Optics</em>, II.3.25). He
included light, color, and visual direction (a correlate of shape)
among the things immediately sensed (Hatfield 2020: 200). The optical
tradition, as promulgated in the medieval European Latin West,
provided a framework for the theories of vision of Descartes and
Berkeley (Hatfield &amp; Epstein 1979: 371). The distinction between
(a) a core of sensation, and (b) perception of objects in the world as
the result of further psychological processes, provided the
predominant framework for theorizing about the senses into the
twentieth
 century.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-4" id="ref-4">4</a>]</sup>
 As promulgated in early modern philosophy and nineteenth-century
British philosophy, it provided the proximate background for the
arguments about sense data just after 1900.</p>

<h3 id="ConcSensDataModePeri">2.1 The Concept of Sense Data in the Modern Period</h3>

<p>
In the second half of the twentieth century, Descartes, Locke,
Berkeley, and Hume were often portrayed as sense-data
 theorists,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-5" id="ref-5">5</a>]</sup>
 especially when suggesting that they abetted skepticism about the
external world. The Scottish philosopher Thomas Reid found that
Hume&rsquo;s external-world skepticism stemmed from his taking the
theory of ideas (initiated by Descartes and adopted by the others) to
its logical conclusion (Nichols &amp; Yaffe 2014 [2016]). Our early
sense-data theorists knew of Reid&rsquo;s interpretation through
studying Reid himself or through the discussion of Reid in William
Hamilton&rsquo;s <em>Lectures on Metaphysics</em> (1861). In the
latter part of the twentieth century, Richard Rorty (1979: ch. 3)
portrayed Descartes and Locke as engendering a veil of perception. He
did not use the term &ldquo;sense data&rdquo; to describe the fabric
of the veil, but he endorsed Reid&rsquo;s charge that the theory of
ideas results in a veil. This reading of Descartes, Locke, Berkeley,
and Hume as proto-sense-data theorists has been challenged (e.g., in
part by Yolton 1984), and the present entry qualifies it in many
respects.</p>

<h4 id="SensDataEarlModePhil">2.1.1 Sense Data and Early Modern Philosophy</h4>

<p>
Several threads within early modern theories of perception provide a
background for the Classical Notion of sense data. First, there was
the already-noted tendency to distinguish sensation from perception,
or a passively received sensory core from a further perceptual act,
involving judgment, inference, or association, which presents objects
as existing in a locally coherent three-dimensional space. Second, the
immediate objects of sensory perception were sometimes rendered as
mental representations from which an external world can be inferred.
This inference might yield knowledge of its objects, though it can
also be seen as facilitating external world skepticism. Third, these
mental representations might or might not be attributed the features
of the Classical Notion. Many early modern theorists, as many
theorists now, distinguished between representation via intentional
contents that do not literally possess the qualities they present
(such as color or shape), and representation through entities that do
possess such properties as they appear (the Classical Notion).</p>

<p>
The terminological distinction between &ldquo;sensation&rdquo; and
&ldquo;perception&rdquo;, explicitly so formulated, is often credited
to Reid, although it is found in Nicolas Malebranche (Simmons 2009)
and (arguably) was already present in Ibn al-Haytham. Taken as a broad
conceptual distinction with several terminological and substantive
variants, it is more widely present. Descartes, in the
<em>Meditations</em> (1641 [1985: 55]), speaks of the perception of
objects as arising from sensory ideas caused by such objects.
Subsequently, in the Sixth Replies, he distinguishes between the
immediate mental products resulting from stimulation in the sense
organs, nerves, and brain, such as perception of an extended area of
light and color, and subsequent inferences or calculations that yield
perception of an object with a specific size, distance, and shape
(1641 [1985: 295]). In other places, Descartes offers a physiological
account of the perception of distance that renders the third dimension
part of the sensory core (<em>Dioptrics</em>, 1637, Sixth Discourse
[1965: 105&ndash;106]; see Hatfield 2020: 212&ndash;213); even so, a
judgment would still be needed for object perception.</p>

<p>
Locke, in the <em>Essay</em> (1690: bk 2, ch. 9, art. 8),
distinguishes between original sensory ideas and the perception of an
object as the result of an unnoticed act of judgment:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
We are farther to consider concerning Perception, that the <em>Ideas
we receive by sensation, are often</em> in grown People
<em>alter&rsquo;d by the Judgment</em>, without our taking notice of
it. When we set before our Eyes a round Globe, of any uniform colour,
<em>v.g.</em> Gold, Alabaster, or Jet, &rsquo;tis certain, that the
<em>Idea</em> thereby imprinted in our Mind, is of a flat Circle
variously shadow&rsquo;d, with several degrees of Light and Brightness
coming to our Eyes. But we having by use been accustomed to perceive,
what kind of appearance convex Bodies are wont to make in us; what
alterations are made in the reflections of Light, by the difference of
the sensible Figures of Bodies, the Judgment presently, by an habitual
custom, alters the Appearances into their Causes: So that from that,
which truly is variety of shadow or colour, collecting the Figure, it
makes it pass for a mark of Figure, and frames to it self the
perception of a convex Figure, and an uniform Colour; when the
<em>Idea</em> we receive from thence, is only a Plain variously
colour&rsquo;d, as is evident in Painting.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
The passage makes a clear distinction between the immediate impression
caused by a spherical object, a flat circle variously colored, and the
subsequent act by which, after a process of learning, the faculty of
judgment &ldquo;frames &hellip; the perception of a convex
Figure&rdquo;. Locke presents the visual experience as flat like a
painting but as containing the structure needed to derive a
three-dimensional perception. In a similar vein, Berkeley
distinguishes between the <em>immediate</em> objects of sight,
including light and color, which form a visible extension with visible
figure and magnitude (varying in two dimensions), and the
<em>mediate</em> objects of sight, which include depth derived from
the sense of touch (<em>Essay Towards a New Theory of Vision</em>,
1709: sec. 49, 55). The immediate object of sight for an approaching
object grows in visible extension. But, as the result of learning
through the sense of touch, the perceiver experiences the mediate
object of sight, an object of constant size.</p>

<p>
Hume offered another view, emphasizing the equivalent of
Berkeley&rsquo;s immediate objects: a table, as ordinarily perceived,
retains a constant size and is thought to persist when not seen. Yet
this ordinary perception is overturned by philosophy, when it notes
that our sensory images of the table vary as we approach or recede
from it and that in fact our experience of the table contains only
these images, and no direct perception of the object
(<em>Enquiry</em>, 1748: XII.I.8&ndash;11).</p>

<p>
These early modern figures have positions consistent with
 <a href="#cn5">item (5) of the Classical Notion</a>,
 as they hold that originally a sensory idea is not linked to an
object beyond it, but only becomes linked through experience. They
might also agree on the privacy of sensations or sensory ideas. The
authors would differ among themselves on other points, such as the
act-object distinction.</p>

<p>
As regards knowledge of external objects, Descartes and Locke were in
fact realists who held that representations mediate the perception of
external objects. Accordingly, perceivers immediately experience
sensory ideas that present properties such as shape and color and,
through an act of judgment, come to perceive external objects as
having properties that cause those sensory
 ideas.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-6" id="ref-6">6</a>]</sup>
 Berkeley and Hume sought to turn this position against them by
arguing that it has skeptical consequences. They sought to avoid such
skepticism by, in Berkeley&rsquo;s case, adopting idealism, which
makes really existent sensory ideas the objects of perception, or, in
Hume&rsquo;s common-sense persona, adopting a common-sense realism
about external objects as (phenomenally) immediately perceived.</p>

<p>
The fact that our early modern authors shared with the Classical
Notion a version of
 <a href="#cn5">item (5)</a>
 does not make them sense-data theorists. Two key ingredients are
items 3 and 6: &ldquo;Sense data have the properties that they appear
to have&rdquo;, and &ldquo;These properties are known to us with
certainty&rdquo;.</p>

<p>
As regards
 <a href="#cn3">item (3)</a>,
 current consensus is that it is not Descartes&rsquo; position. He did
not believe that states of mind such as sensory ideas have properties
of color or other sensory qualities. Rather, color and other qualities
are in the mind &ldquo;representatively&rdquo;, or in terms of
&ldquo;objective being&rdquo; or &ldquo;objective reality&rdquo;
(<em>Meditations</em>, 1641 [1985: 28&ndash;29, 74&ndash;75,
113&ndash;114]). Descartes is here understood to be invoking a notion
of intentionality or intentional
 being.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-7" id="ref-7">7</a>]</sup>
 The phenomenal red that one experiences is not an instance of the
property <em>red</em> that is predicated of the mind, but rather the
idea of red has an intentional content that is experienced as
phenomenal red (Yolton 1984: ch. 1; 1996: 74&ndash;75; Nadler 2006:
91&ndash;92). Similarly, the mind does not itself become square when
it experiences a square.</p>

<p>
With Locke, perusal of the <em>Essay</em>, bk 2, ch. 8, indicates that
he is not clear on the matter. Interpreters find two distinct notions
of &ldquo;sensory ideas&rdquo; in Locke: as objects of perception that
might potentially instantiate color and shape, like sense data; or as
acts of perceiving that might exhibit color and shape only by way of
representation (Yolton 1984: ch. 3), understood here to mean through
nonconceptual intentional content (see footnote 7).</p>

<p>
Although Berkeley is sometimes classified as an early sense-data
theorist, the issue is complex. On the one hand, he claims to be a
champion of common sense who &ldquo;believes [his] senses&rdquo;, that
is, who holds that &ldquo;the real things are those very things I see
and feel&rdquo; and that &ldquo;colours and other sensible qualities
are on the objects&rdquo; (<em>Three Dialogues</em>, 1713, Third
Dialogue [2008, 210]). Berkeley holds that we are immediately aware
only of sensory ideas, and passages such as these might suggest that
those ideas bear property-instances of red or other qualities. On the
other hand, he speaks of light and color as &ldquo;passions or
sensations in the soul&rdquo; (<em>Three Dialogues</em>, First
Dialogue [2008: 178]), describes figures and colors as &ldquo;sensible
appearances&rdquo;, and equates them with sensory &ldquo;ideas&rdquo;
(<em>Three Dialogues</em>, First Dialogue [2008: 185]).</p>

<p>
Berkeley addressed the question of whether sensory ideas have the
properties they exhibit in defending his idealistic view that only
immaterial minds and ideas exist. If minds are immaterial and have no
extension, how can we have an idea of an extended thing, for instance,
a square? Here Berkeley in effect invoked the Cartesian notion of
objective reality or (in present-day terms) intentional content. When
challenged with the claim that</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
if extension and figure exist only in the mind, it follows that the
mind is extended and figured; since extension is a mode or
attribute,</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
he answered:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
those qualities are in the mind only as they are perceived by it, that
is, not by way of <em>mode</em> or <em>attribute</em>, but only by way
of <em>idea</em>; and it no more follows, that the soul or mind is
extended because extension exists in it alone, than it does that it is
red or blue, because these colours are on all hands acknowledged to
exist in it. (<em>Principles</em>, pt 1, art. 49; see also <em>Three
Dialogues</em>, Third Dialogue [2008, 229&ndash;230])</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Unlike sense data, the sensory idea of an extended shape or a color
does not have the shape or color as a property; rather, perceived
shapes and colors are a kind of intentional content, a way in which
things
 appear.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-8" id="ref-8">8</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
As regards
 <a href="#cn6">item (6)</a>,
 that perceivers enjoy certainty in the apprehension of sensory ideas,
a standard view has Descartes and Locke asserting or seeking such
certainty (Rorty 1979: chs. 1, 3). This reading has been challenged
for Descartes, Locke, and Berkeley (Yolton 1984: ch. 5; Hatfield
2011). Accordingly, hope for an early modern expression of items (3) and
(6) of the Classical Notion must look to Hume.</p>

<p>
Hume held a version of the sense-datum theory, which treated such data
as mental or phenomenal items. He provided what became the first
standard argument for the existence of sense data, the Argument from
Perceptual Variation.</p>

<p>
In <em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em> (1748), Hume
considers the common-sense view that we directly perceive material
objects. This sort of na&iuml;ve realism is, Hume says,</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
destroyed by the slightest philosophy, which teaches us, that nothing
can ever be present to the mind but an image or perception, and that
the senses are the only inlets, through which these images are
conveyed. (<em>Enquiry</em>, XII.I.9)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
He then argues:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
The table, which we see, seems to diminish, as we move farther from
it: But the real table, which exists independent of us, suffers no
alteration: It was, therefore, nothing but its image, which was
present to the mind.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
As Reid (<em>Intellectual Powers</em>, 1785: Essay 2, ch. 14) was to
point out, if we take &ldquo;seems&rdquo; here to refer to an
appearance of the table itself, then there is no contradiction:
appearances of a thing can vary even if the thing does not. But we
should treat &ldquo;seems&rdquo; as an instance of what Chisholm
(1957: 43&ndash;44) calls its &ldquo;epistemic&rdquo; use. To say that
a thing seems to diminish in size is to affirm that it does diminish
in size. Accordingly, phenomenal size reveals something that actually
varies in size. We may then formulate the argument as:</p>

<ol type="1">

<li>The table that I see diminishes in size as we move away from
it.</li>

<li>The real table does not change size as we move away from it.</li>

<li>Therefore, the table that I see is not the real table.</li>
</ol>

<p>
What Hume perceives are just sensory images or sense impressions (a
version of
 <a href="#cn1">item (1)</a>).
 Because the object seen diminishes but the real table does not, our
sensory experience acquaints us not with the real table but with a
mental or phenomenal
 impression.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-9" id="ref-9">9</a>]</sup>
 As a result, Hume held that we cannot know an external world of
mind-independent objects, such as the purported &ldquo;real
table&rdquo;, because our sensory knowledge is limited to sequences of
sense impressions.</p>

<p>
Thus far, we have not found explicit endorsement of items (3) and (6).
These items are found most clearly in Hume&rsquo;s <em>Treatise of
Human Nature</em> (1739&ndash;40). He there says that &ldquo;every
impression&rdquo;, including sense impressions, &ldquo;appear, all of
them, in their true colours, as impressions or perceptions&rdquo;
(<em>Treatise</em>, I.4.2.7; again, Chisholm&rsquo;s epistemic
&ldquo;appear&rdquo;). But, further,</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
since all actions and sensations of the mind are known to us by
consciousness, they must necessarily appear in every particular what
they are, and be what they appear.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Otherwise, we must suppose</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
that even where we are most intimately conscious, we might be
mistaken.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Hume here endorses positions otherwise known as the
&ldquo;transparency&rdquo; of sensory experiences and the
&ldquo;incorrigibility&rdquo; and of our knowledge of them: if our
impressions have a property, that property must appear to us
(transparency), and we cannot be mistaken about how our impressions
appear and so how they are (incorrigibility). This holds of
extension:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
the very idea of extension is copy&rsquo;d from nothing but an
impression, and consequently must perfectly agree to it. To say the
idea of extension agrees to any thing, is to say it is extended.
(<em>Treatise</em>, I.4.5.15)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
An impression of a square, which we are conscious of as extended, is
really extended. Our consciousness of sensory properties as present to
us phenomenally reveals the properties as they are. Hume affirms items
(3) and (6). Indeed, substituting &ldquo;impression&rdquo;,
&ldquo;idea&rdquo;, and &ldquo;perception&rdquo; for &ldquo;sense
datum&rdquo;, we might ascribe to Hume all of the items in the
Classical Notion, save perhaps
 <a href="#cn8">item (8)</a>,
 the act-object relation. One might be tempted to ascribe item (8) to
Hume, but he notoriously rejected such ascriptions in the Appendix to
the <em>Treatise</em>.</p>

<p>
The problem of the external world was a legacy of early modern
philosophy for the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. At Cambridge
and Oxford, into the 1890s discussions of this problem referenced
Hamilton&rsquo;s <em>Lectures on Metaphysics</em> (1861) and J. S.
Mill&rsquo;s <em>An Examination of Sir William Hamilton&rsquo;s
Philosophy</em> (1872). In that decade and the next, G. F. Stout and
James Ward at Cambridge discussed the problem as did Thomas Case and
John Cook Wilson at Oxford (Nasim 2008, Hatfield 2013b).</p>

<h4 id="HamiSensData">2.1.2 Hamilton, Mill, and Sense Data</h4>

<p>
Hamilton was Professor of Logic and Metaphysics at Edinburgh and
regularly gave lectures in each subject area. In his <em>Lectures on
Metaphysics</em>, he offered a taxonomy of positions on the problem of
the immediate objects of perception. This taxonomy distinguished those
who accepted or rejected what Hamilton considered to be the
fundamental &ldquo;fact of consciousness&rdquo; or &ldquo;datum of
consciousness&rdquo; (1861, 2: 86). In Hamilton&rsquo;s view,</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
consciousness gives, as an ultimate fact, a primitive duality; a
knowledge of the ego in relation and contrast to the non-ego; and a
knowledge of the non-ego in relation and contrast to the ego. (1861,
1: 292)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Further, it is &ldquo;given&rdquo; that ego and non-ego are mind and
matter and that these are co-equal. Hamilton called his position
&ldquo;Natural Realism&rdquo; or &ldquo;Natural Dualism&rdquo;.
Affirming the datum of consciousness, he contended more specifically
that some portion of the external world itself is given to the subject
in immediate perception. In vision, the physical retinal image is an
immediately given material entity (1861, 2: 130, 179&ndash;184).
Accordingly, we have intuitive knowledge of a two-dimensional image,
which we are able to understand as a projection of the
three-dimensional physical world. This position would allow Hamilton
to reject Hume&rsquo;s Argument from Perceptual Variation, because the
&ldquo;real object&rdquo; of perception, the retinal image, does vary
as we approach or recede from the real table. We don&rsquo;t
immediately perceive the real table but instead infer it from
something that we do perceive immediately: the retinal image. This
image plays the role later occupied by sense
 data.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-10" id="ref-10">10</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
Hamilton also described positions that emended or rejected his
fundamental datum of consciousness. Some rejected the datum and held
that we immediately perceive something that differs from the external
world. Hamilton called this a &ldquo;representative&rdquo; theory
(1861, 1: 295), or, more impressively, he named it &ldquo;Hypothetical
Dualism&rdquo; or &ldquo;Cosmothetic Idealism&rdquo;. Holders of this
position are subdivided</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
into those who view in the object immediately perceived, a <em>tertium
quid</em> different both from the external reality and from the
conscious mind, and into those who identify this object with a
modification of the mind itself. (1861, 1: 298)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Hamilton&rsquo;s two versions of Hypothetical Dualism posit
sense-data-like representative entities, either as a <em>tertium
quid</em> or as a mental representation. He effectively qualifies the
Classical Notion by suggesting that, in the positions he is
discussing, sense data intrinsically represent. It is unclear whether
the posited mental representations have the properties they seem to
have
 [<a href="#cn3">item (3)</a>].</p>
 
<p>
Turning to John Stuart Mill, he argued against the position,
maintained by Hamilton (and later adopted by a minority of sense-data
theorists), that in perception we are intuitively aware of a portion
of the material world. Mill did not deny Hamilton&rsquo;s premise that
whatever is immediately and intuitively known in consciousness is
known with certainty (<em>Examination</em>, 158), but he contended
that some states of consciousness that seem intuitive, such as the
allegedly intuitive experience of a material world, are actually the
result of previous psychological processes of habit formation through
association. These contents of consciousness would accordingly be
complex products, not intuitively given primary data.</p>

<p>
Mill attacked Hamilton&rsquo;s datum as a basis for knowing the
external world. Hamilton purported to find material existence through
a simple and ineliminable (or &ldquo;necessary&rdquo;) fact of
consciousness. Mill replied, grouping Hamilton together with Reid,
Kant, and others:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
The test by which they all decide a belief to be a part of our
primitive consciousness&mdash;an original intuition of the
mind&mdash;is the necessity of thinking it. Their proof that we must
always, from the beginning, have had the belief, is the impossibility
of getting rid of it now. This argument, applied to any of the
disputed questions of philosophy, is doubly illegitimate: neither the
major nor the minor premise is admissible. For ... the very fact that
the questions are disputed, disproves the alleged impossibility.
(<em>Examination</em>, 181&ndash;182)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Finding that a belief has been entrenched through past experience
unmasks it as merely habit-based and not a product of a
necessity-bestowing intuition. We&rsquo;ll return to this distinction
below, anent Russell&rsquo;s logical and psychological &ldquo;hard
data&rdquo;.</p>

<h3 id="FormFlouConcTermSensData">2.2 Formation and Flourishing of the Concept and Term &ldquo;Sense Data&rdquo;</h3>

<p>
The elevation of the concept and term &ldquo;sense data&rdquo; is
standardly credited to G. E. Moore and Bertrand
 Russell.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-11" id="ref-11">11</a>]</sup>
 The relevant background includes not only the problem of the external
world but also their own early and joint commitment to British
Idealism. The affirmation of sense data denied a central tenet of such
idealism, that perception yields no knowledge because it is always an
incomplete grasping of the whole or the Absolute. As Russell put it,
looking back from 1943:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
Bradley argued that everything common sense believes in is mere
appearance; we reverted to the opposite extreme, and thought that
<em>everything</em> is real that common sense, uninfluenced by
philosophy or theology, supposes real. With a sense of escaping from
prison, we allowed ourselves to think that grass is green, that the
sun and stars would exist if no one was aware of them, and also that
there is a pluralistic timeless world of Platonic ideas. (1951:
12)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
As we shall see, the realisms that Russell constructs are in some
respects peculiar, for one version denies mind-independent material
things without embracing idealism.</p>

<p>
Moore developed the concept of sense data first. His conception
primarily focused on epistemology, whereas Russell combined
epistemology with various metaphysical schemes for building from sense
 data.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-12" id="ref-12">12</a>]</sup></p>
 
<h4 id="MoorSensData">2.2.1 Moore and Sense Data</h4>

<p>
Moore broke with idealism in 1898 and his &ldquo;Refutation of
Idealism&rdquo; appeared in 1903. This paper did not introduce the
concept of sense data, but it formulated the important distinction, to
which he remained loyal, between the act of sensation and its object.
Moore contended that every case of sensation involves awareness of two
distinct elements, one common to all sensations and the other
differing among them. He distinguished the common element, which he
called &ldquo;consciousness&rdquo;, from the differing
&ldquo;objects&rdquo; of sensation, which might be blue in one case,
green in another. In his conception, consciousness is the same at all
times and the objects provide consciousness with content. He did not
claim to prove that the object is what it seems to be: a material
object at a distance, but he endorsed a na&iuml;ve realism, according
to which we are directly aware of &ldquo;the existence of a table in
space&rdquo; just as we are directly aware of the conscious element
(1903: 453).</p>

<p>
In December, 1909, Moore gave a paper at the Aristotelian Society on
&ldquo;The Subject-Matter of Psychology&rdquo; in which he introduced
the term &ldquo;sense
 data&rdquo;.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-13" id="ref-13">13</a>]</sup>
 The paper again divided &ldquo;mental acts&rdquo; from their objects:
when we perceive one color and then another, the act of consciousness
is of the same type, whereas the colors that are objects of the act
differ. Such acts of consciousness can themselves differ in quality
(one mental act may be a perceiving, another a willing, etc.), and
many mental acts may be conjoined to form a complex mental entity. But
such acts are distinct from their objects, even if those objects exist
only when we perceive them, as, Moore reports, some people believe
about colors. He then introduces the term &ldquo;sense
data&rdquo;:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
By sense-data I understand a class of entities of which we are very
often directly conscious, and with many of which we are extremely
familiar. They include the colours, of all sorts of different shades,
which I actually see when I look about me; the sounds which I actually
hear; the peculiar sort of entity of which I am directly conscious
when I feel the pain of a toothache, and which I call &ldquo;the
pain&rdquo;; and many others which I need not enumerate. But I wish
also to include among them those entities called &ldquo;images&rdquo;,
of which I am directly conscious when I dream and often also when
awake; which resemble the former in respect of the fact that they
<em>are</em> colours, sounds, etc.; but which seem, as a rule, like
rather faint copies of the colours, sounds, etc., actually seen or
heard, and which, whether fainter or not, differ from them in respect
of the fact that we should not say we actually saw or heard them, and
the fact that they are not, in the strictest sense of the words,
&ldquo;given by the senses&rdquo;. All these entities I propose to
call sense-data. And in their case there is, of course, no question
whether there <em>are</em> such entities. The entities meant certainly
<em>are</em>, whether or not they be rightly described as
&ldquo;sensations&rdquo;, &ldquo;sense-presentations&rdquo;,
&ldquo;sense-data&rdquo;, etc. Here the only question can be, whether
they are &ldquo;mental&rdquo;. (1909&ndash;10: 57)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Moore&rsquo;s remarks are not in the spirit of <em>arguing</em> that
sense data exist but of <em>drawing our attention</em> to their
obvious existence; his argument concerns whether they are mental.
Moore observes (1909&ndash;10: 58) that philosophers such as Stout
regard sense data as mental because they fail to distinguish act from
object, consciousness of blue from the blue of which we are conscious.
Having made this distinction between act and object, he finds it
manifest that the object is non-mental (1909&ndash;10:
58&ndash;60).</p>

<p>
Moore intended the term &ldquo;sense data&rdquo; to provide a neutral
description for whatever is the immediate object of our awareness and
hence to be neutral on the problem of the external world. He proposed
and revamped proposals about sense data over his entire career, as he
analyzed and re-analyzed instances of perception, their careful
description, and their contribution to knowing. For half a century, he
considered various theories about how sense data might relate to
physical objects. In these discussions, he sometimes used
&ldquo;sensibles&rdquo; as a broader term that includes unsensed
sense-data (1913&ndash;14: 357&ndash;358). Without tracking the
various shifts in his preferred theory, we can note that he considered
five relations that sense data might have to physical objects or other
actual or potential causes (Moore 1913&ndash;14, 1918&ndash;19, 1925,
1957). On one conception, physical objects might be regarded as</p>

<ol start="1" type="1">

<li id="pos1">collections of sensed and unsensed sense data,
independent of any conception of the causal source of such data.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Bringing in potential causes of sense data, these might be:</p>

<ol start="2" type="1">

<li id="pos2">a spiritual entity, as in Berkeleyan idealism;</li>

<li id="pos3">an unknown thing in itself; or</li>

<li id="pos4">physical objects, as in Locke&rsquo;s view of
representative realism.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Finally, a sense datum might be</p>

<ol start="5" type="1">

<li id="pos5">a part of the surface of a physical object.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Moore (1913&ndash;14: 376) identified (1) with Russell and Mill (e.g.,
Russell 1914a, b) and allowed that it might be right. But he favored
positions (4) and (5). In (4), sense data stand in a relation to
physical objects, called &ldquo;relation R&rdquo;, which turns out to
be a representational relation (Moore 1925, 1957). In (5), the
perceiver is directly aware of a part of the surface of a physical
object, which makes this position a form of direct realism.</p>

<p>
In discussing sense data, Moore introduced some facts that illustrate
the relativity of perception, using coins (1913&ndash;14:
371&ndash;372). With U.S. coins, a penny is physically smaller than a
quarter and both are round. Moore and other observers report that when
looking at the coins from a position other than directly above them,
they appear more or less elliptical, becoming thin rectangles when
seen edge-on. Further, the quarter, seen at a sufficiently greater
distance than the penny, appears smaller than it. Moore wanted to
account for these purported facts that exhibit perceptual relativity,
while preserving the common-sense beliefs that we really do, on some
occasions, see coins; that the coins really are round; that they have
an inside and a lower side, even though these aren&rsquo;t presently
seen; that the quarter is really larger than the penny; and that the
coins exist when we aren&rsquo;t looking at them.</p>

<p>
 <a href="#pos4">Position (4)</a>,
 a form of representative
 realism,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-14" id="ref-14">14</a>]</sup>
 accounts for the perceptual relativity that makes us want to
distinguish the immediate object of perception from the properties we
ascribe to physical objects (Perceptual Variation). The immediate
object is elliptical; the penny, round. Although accommodating
relativity, this position may be challenged for making it difficult to
know the properties of the physical object, the object being once
removed from perception. But Moore (1913&ndash;14: 379&ndash;380) was
not swayed by this challenge, generally aligning himself with common
sense beliefs that physical objects exist and are knowable.</p>

<p>
 <a href="#pos5">Position (5)</a>
 equates the sense datum with a portion of the surface of the physical
object and indicates that this portion provides the datum by being
directly sensed. When viewed, a portion of the physical surface is the
datum, from which perception of the whole object may follow. But when
given part of the surface of an object, such as an inkstand, the
perceiver does not thereby perceive the whole inkstand, which would
require a further act (Moore1918&ndash;19).</p>

<p>
In considering this view, Moore brought out a special feature. For the
other positions, if the appearance changes, the sense datum has
changed. For example, a sense datum might change its size, and be
perceived to have done so, when an object is made more distant. If we
take Position 5, the portion of the surface of the physical inkstand
that is given does not diminish with distance, since physical surfaces
themselves do not regularly diminish when viewed from farther way.
Similarly, if a portion of the physical surface of a coin is given,
and the coin is first seen straight on and then at an angle, the other
theories say that the datum itself changes (from being round to being
elliptical). But under Position 5, the datum doesn&rsquo;t change, it
only appears to do so.</p>

<p>
Now Moore has a problem. To account for perceptual relativity, he
(1918&ndash;19: 23&ndash;24) violates a basic tenet concerning sense
data: that we perceive them as they are
 [<a href="#cn3">item (3) of the Classical Notion</a>].
 If the penny is round and we are directly aware of its physical
surface, even when viewed at an angle, we are perceiving a round
thing. But, Moore holds, the round thing is perceived to
&ldquo;seem&rdquo; elliptical. It is not perceived to <em>be</em>
elliptical, as in the other four positions on sense data, and as Moore
himself had previously assumed (1918&ndash;19: 23). Hence, in holding
(5), one must distinguish the actual properties of the directly seen
surface from the properties it seems to have, such as seeming to be
elliptical (1918&ndash;19: 24). There is no actually elliptical datum.
Moore did his best to uphold the direct realism of (5) and at times
favored it. But in his final publication (1957), he endorsed
 <a href="#pos4">(4)</a>
 instead.</p>

<h4 id="RussSensData">2.2.2 Russell and Sense Data</h4>

<p>
In 1911&ndash;1912, Russell returned to a project he had started soon
after the break from Idealism, investigating the concept of matter. A
need to first secure the foundations of mathematics had diverted him
from this project (and resulted in <em>Principia</em>). Now, from 1912
on, he approached the analysis of matter from the perspectives of
epistemology and ontology, looking into our knowledge and conception
of the fundamental constituents of the external world. The main events
concerning sense data proper run from the <em>Problems of
Philosophy</em> (1912) to the <em>Analysis of Mind</em> (1921),
although the legacy of sense data extends further.</p>

<p>
Russell&rsquo;s initial discussions in this period prioritized
epistemology. Consider the opening sentence of his 1912 popular book,
<em>The Problems of Philosophy</em>:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
Is there any knowledge in the world which is so certain that no
reasonable man could doubt it? (1912 [1997: 7])</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
The first positive instance he offers is knowledge of sense data.</p>

<p>
Russell finds that when he looks at an object such as a table, his
perception of the table does not reveal the actual properties of the
table but rather a series of sensations that vary in what they
present. The character of color sensations change as one moves around
a table, sometimes being brown, but in cases of reflected light (say,
from a window), giving a sensation of white. The shape of the table
appears as a sequence of quadrilaterals; viewed from one end, it
appears as a regular trapezoid, and the physically parallel sides
yield a sense experience of convergence. Russell uses the Argument
from Perceptual Variation to establish sense data (1912 [1997:
10&ndash;11]):</p>

<ol type="1">

<li>In looking at a table, what we see varies in shape as we
move.</li>

<li>The real table does not vary in shape.</li>

<li>Hence, what we see is not the real table.</li>
</ol>

<p>
He offers similar arguments for color and felt properties such as
hardness.</p>

<p>
Russell gives the name &ldquo;sense data&rdquo; to</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
the things that are immediately known in sensation: such things as
colours, sounds, smells, hardnesses, roughnesses, and so on. (1912
[1997: 12])</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
In further discussion, he sustains the eight elements of the Classical
Notion. He further suggests that sense data may exist only when sensed
(1912 [1997: 23]). Like Moore, he emphasizes the importance of
distinguishing between the mental act of sensation and the object of
that act (1912 [1997: 12]). The act is mental, the object need not be.
Again like Moore, he contends that believing that sense data are
mental arises from failing to distinguish act from object (1912 [1997:
41&ndash;43]).</p>

<p>
Russell further asks whether the table is real and, if so, what it
might be (1912 [1997: 11, 19&ndash;25]). He answers that the table
most likely is a real material object, rectangular and brown.
Accordingly, he assigns to material objects the role of causing sense
data. Sense data then &ldquo;represent&rdquo; the objects that cause
them (1912 [1997: 23]). This is not part of the base concept of sense
data but is the result of further analysis. He has posited sense data
as &ldquo;third things&rdquo;, standing between material objects and
perceivers, and serving as the immediate objects of perception.</p>

<p>
In <em>Problems</em>, Russell distinguished knowledge by acquaintance,
which involves a direct relation to sense data, from knowledge by
description or &ldquo;knowledge that&rdquo;. Although bare
acquaintance makes us aware of the properties of sense data, it does
not assert any truths or involve any judgments, hence does not
constitute &ldquo;knowledge that&rdquo; something is the case (1912
[1997: 46]). We come to know basic facts about sense data by making
judgments about them. In obtaining sense data of a brown table, we
easily come to know that we are seeing something brown, or,
subsequently, a whitish sheen, or an appearance of one or another
trapezoidal surface. This is what Russell calls &ldquo;knowledge of
things by <em>description</em>&rdquo; (1912 [1997: 46]). Description
is only possible if we have access to universals, such as brownness,
whiteness, being a surface, being a trapezoid, and so on. According to
Russell, repeated sensations allow us to become acquainted with
sensory universals:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
When we see a white patch, we are acquainted, in the first instance,
with the particular patch; but by seeing many white patches, we easily
learn to abstract the whiteness which they all have in common, and in
learning to do this we are learning to be acquainted with whiteness.
(1912 [1997: 101])</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Having acquaintance with universals such as whiteness, we can make
judgments that yield knowledge by description of the properties of
things. These are judgments of fact about sense data, which establish
truths.</p>

<p>
Going forward, Russell retained the view that sense data (or their
counterparts) provide epistemological bedrock. But within a year or
so, in <em>Our Knowledge of the External World</em> (1914a) and
&ldquo;The Relation of Sense-data to Physics&rdquo; (1914b), he
changed position on the metaphysics of sense data in relation to
material objects. He came to view such objects as logical
constructions out of sense data. He found no need to assert the
existence of material objects, whether the substantial
&ldquo;thing&rdquo; of common sense or the atoms and molecules of
physics (1914b: 109, 114&ndash;115). Rather, he populated the world
with minds and sense data and new entities termed
&ldquo;sensibilia&rdquo;. Things and micro-physical particles became
logical constructions, not inferred entities as in his representative
realism of 1912. This adjustment may be seen as an extension of his
recognition that sense data are very well known. Treating material
objects as mere logical constructions avoids a shaky inference from
well-known data to the actual existence of physical things.</p>

<p>
Russell retained and clarified his conception of occurrent sense data,
including the eight points of the Classical Notion. He retained the
distinction between mental act and non-mental object (1914a: 76;
1914b: 113). Sense data are non-mental, but they exist as sense data
only while they are present to the mind (1914a: 64; 1914b: 110). He
now identified two additional entities, inferred rather than
constructed, that would figure into his account of the external world:
other people&rsquo;s sense data, inferred from their testimony and
ultimately relying on analogy with one&rsquo;s own mind to posit other
minds; and</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
the &ldquo;sensiblia&rdquo; which would appear from places where there
happen to be no minds, and which I suppose to be real although they
are no one&rsquo;s data. (1914b: 116)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Sensibilia, as postulated entities, are particulars that become sense
data when a perceiver enters into a relation of acquaintance with one
of them. Thus, there are many potential trapezoidal appearances as of
a table. When someone has a sequence of table-like experiences, some
of those trapezoidal appearances are manifest as sense data. At other
perspectives, not occupied by any observer, there are unsensed
particulars. As Russell put it:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
I shall give the name <em>sensibilia</em> to those objects which have
the same metaphysical and physical status as sense-data without
necessarily being data to any mind. (1914b: 110)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
The notion of &ldquo;object&rdquo; here is not that of an ordinary
physical object. Rather, as the objects of sensation, Russell posits a
bevy of fleeting particulars, the exact time course of which he
didn&rsquo;t specify.</p>

<p>
Russell&rsquo;s analysis of our perception of an external world
advanced in other ways. In <em>Problems</em> (1912: ch. 1), he had
noted that we are so accustomed to judging the &ldquo;real&rdquo;
shapes of things that we believe &ldquo;that we actually see the real
shapes&rdquo; (1912 [1997: 10]), that is, we uncritically assume that
we see the table as rectangular. But when we adopt the attitude needed
to produce a drawing of the table, we may come to notice the
appearances of various trapezoidal shapes. In <em>Our Knowledge</em>
(1914a: ch. 3), Russell further analyzes this difference. His analysis
depends on the relation between what is logically and psychologically
primitive or else derivative. Logically primitive beliefs are those
that are not logically derived via inference or construction. In order
to strengthen our knowledge, we seek to align these primitive beliefs
with the psychologically primitive. While having an ordinary
perception of the table with its actual shape, we mistakenly believe
that this experience, which has resulted from a learned psychological
process, is psychologically primitive; accordingly, we also designate
this perception of the table as logically primitive. Scientific
psychology now offers a point that Russell had attributed to learning
to draw: that what is actually psychologically primitive is the
perspectival view of the table as trapezoidal (1914a: 68). Armed with
this psychological finding, we endeavor to make the trapezoidal datum
logically primitive (1914a: 69&ndash;70). As with Mill&rsquo;s
apparently but not actually intuitive beliefs, before epistemological
analysis many beliefs about sensory things are logically primitive,
because not reached through logical derivation, and psychologically
derivative, because caused by extra-logical psychological processes
such as association. Upon further consideration, we favor beliefs that
are both psychologically and logically primitive (1914a: 69&ndash;70)
and so establish sense data as logically primitive.</p>

<p>
Russell&rsquo;s postulation of sensibilia allowed him to achieve
greater metaphysical regularity and economy. Sensibilia make it that
the objects of sense, color patches and like, do not simply go out of
existence when unobserved. Rather, sensibilia persist and we sample
them when we occupy positions that present specific
table-perspectives. But, further, he economizes by replacing the
&ldquo;real&rdquo; physical table with sense data and sensibilia at
various locations. These are coordinated between the senses, and so
include those as of approaching the table with our eye until our face
touches it, a perspective that connects with the felt location of
table (1914b: 119&ndash;120).</p>

<p>
By 1920, Russell proposed even greater ontological economy by giving
up mental acts and the experiencing subject as distinct from sense
data. This meant that the &ldquo;data&rdquo; were no longer given to a
distinct subject and so were improperly called &ldquo;sense
data&rdquo;; henceforth, he spoke of &ldquo;momentary
particulars&rdquo; or &ldquo;events&rdquo;. He explicated this
&ldquo;neutral monism&rdquo; in the <em>Analysis of Mind</em> (1921:
ch. 1). He saw himself as adopting James&rsquo;s (1904) position,
according to which both the self and the external world are composed
of one neutral stuff&mdash;momentary particulars, characterized by
perceptual qualities such as color and shape (for visual particulars).
Accordingly, he discarded the act-object analysis of mind and sense
perception, as there is no longer recognition of a mind that acts but
only of various sequences of momentary particulars. The sequence that
includes all the experiences that I&rsquo;ve had today (and before)
constitutes my mind, another sequence of such particulars constitutes
your mind. Physical objects, such as a table, become a different
sequence of particulars, which are like sensibilia in giving
perspectives on a table or a room that is composed of them. If no
one&rsquo;s sequence of particulars intersects with those of a
particular classroom, then that room and its contents consist of a
series of momentary particulars now occurring without anyone present.
The material entities of physics are, as before, treated as
constructions from momentary
 particulars.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-15" id="ref-15">15</a>]</sup></p>
 
<h4 id="FurtDeveClasNotiSensData">2.2.3 Further Development of the Classical Notion of Sense Data</h4>

<p>
Many other classical theorists accepted the view that sense data are
non-mental. Broad (1923) and Price (1932) were realists who
acknowledged both sense data and material objects. Broad explored a
&ldquo;critical scientific realism&rdquo; by elaborating the notion of
sense data (or, in his terms, &ldquo;sensa&rdquo;) from Russell and
others. His position was in some ways similar to traditional
representative realism, but Broad denied that sensa are themselves
mental states, in the sense of being dependent on the mind for their
existence (1923: 265&ndash;266), and, further, he denied that physical
objects are inferred from sensa. Rather, sensing sensa induces an
underived belief in mind-independent physical objects, a belief that
is not reached by inference and cannot be justified by inference
(1923: 268). Broad agreed with Russell&rsquo;s point that, in ordinary
perceptual consciousness, sense data are not manifest but may become
so through an act of attention. He rejected the picture that we focus
on sensa first and then explicitly infer objects and their properties
from them; rather, we &ldquo;pass automatically&rdquo;, by a primitive
feature of our psychology, from sensa (perhaps unnoticed) to judgments
about objects and their properties (1923: 246&ndash;248).</p>

<p>
Price&rsquo;s book, <em>Perception</em> (1932), is an especially
probing account of sense data, their ontological status and cognitive
role. He begins by isolating a sense datum as what is given in
perception such that we cannot doubt its existence: the extant bulgy
patch of red that occurs when a tomato is viewed in good light cannot
be doubted, even if we can doubt whether we are seeing a tomato or
indeed any physical object at all (1932: 3). The bulgy patch is
directly present in consciousness, that is, is &ldquo;given&rdquo; as
a datum, by means of a relation of mental acquaintance. As mentioned,
Price recommended that the term &ldquo;sense data&rdquo; should be
considered, in its meaning, neutral on ontology. But he went on to
argue for a specific conception of what sense data are, including a
specification of their cognitive status and their ontology as a
<em>tertium quid</em>.</p>

<p>
Against the &ldquo;causal&rdquo; theory of perception (effectively,
representative realism), Price argues that the concept of cause is
neither necessary nor sufficient for our belief in physical objects;
that, even in spatial perception, sense data resemble physical objects
only in some cases; and that, in any case, material objects are not
inferred from sense data. He further maintains that although sense
data are distinct from material objects (contra na&iuml;ve realism)
and their existence may require the existence of minds, they are not
mental (1932: 117&ndash;127, 316&ndash;317). He elaborates notions of
perceptual acceptance and perceptual assurance to describe the
non-inferential cognitive acts through which we accept and believe
that material objects exist. The belief in material objects consists
in (a) the construction of a &ldquo;standard solid&rdquo; from sense
data, which is a spatial structure that fits the outer shape of a
physical object in three dimensions, together with (b) belief in a
causally efficacious &ldquo;physical occupant&rdquo; of this location
(1932: ch.
 8).<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-16" id="ref-16">16</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
Price (1932: ch. 2, esp. pp. 27&ndash;33) produced a version of the
renowned Argument from Illusion as supporting sense data in opposition
to na&iuml;ve realism. In this argument, if the penny looks elliptical
or the table trapezoidal, these are illusions, since the penny is
round and the table rectangular. Price&rsquo;s version of the Argument
may be formulated as
 follows:<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-17" id="ref-17">17</a>]</sup></p>
 
<ol type="1">

<li id="arg1">In viewing a soccer ball in the distance, we are
acquainted with a flat circle.</li>

<li id="arg2">The surface of the soccer ball itself is spherical.</li>

<li id="arg3">Hence, what we are acquainted with is not the surface of
the soccer ball. Call the object of acquaintance a sense datum.</li>

<li id="arg4">The sense datum is illusory: the ball is spherical, not
flat.</li>

<li id="arg5">In walking toward and drawing near to the soccer ball,
we are now acquainted with a bulgy, hemispherical surface; our
perception is now veridical.</li>

<li id="arg6">There is a continuous series of perceptions of the
ball&rsquo;s shape as we walk, transitioning from flat to
hemispherical.</li>

<li id="arg7">Our perceptual consciousness of this series exhibits no
qualitative, phenomenal breaks when transitioning.</li>

<li id="arg8">If there is no phenomenal break then there is no
qualitative difference in the veridical and illusory experiences
considered as experiences.</li>

<li id="arg9">It is not plausible that qualitatively similar
perceptions are of radically different objects, the actual surface of
the ball in the near case and an illusory sense datum in the distant
and in-between cases.</li>

<li id="arg10">Since I am surely acquainted with a sense datum in the
distant case, I am acquainted with a sense datum throughout.</li>

<li id="arg11">Hence, the direct object of perception is a sense
datum.</li>
</ol>

<p>
The argument relies on the indistinguishability of far and near
perceptual experiences, in the sense that there is no experiential
mark or distinguishing characteristic within the experiences that
indicates that the near experience is veridical and the far experience
is not. Price argues that since the far experience does not directly
present the object&rsquo;s surface and shape, the relevantly
indistinguishable near experience also does not directly present that
surface and shape. Price observes that experiences of the ball&rsquo;s
shape vary continuously with distance, which tells against the claim
that nearby experiences of the ball are direct while other experiences
are mediated by sense
 data.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-18" id="ref-18">18</a>]</sup>
 He extended the argument to include hallucinations (1932:
28&ndash;29), which are explained as the having of sense data that are
indistinguishable from veridical perception in that there is no
experiential character that indicates that the one is veridical and
the other not. The finding that the various perceptual experiences are
relevantly indistinguishable supports sense data in all three cases:
veridical perception, illusion, and hallucination.</p>

<p>
In subsequent discussions, this argument received much attention (see
Ayer 1940: ch. 1; Robinson 1994: ch. 2; for an overview, Smith 2002:
ch. 1). In contrast, when Russell, Moore, Broad, and others spoke of
Perceptual Variation and the relativity of perception, they did not
treat the elliptical appearance of a penny as illusory. They simply
affirmed that, from various positions, the penny looks elliptical even
though it is round. Further, although they may have endorsed the
phenomenal indistinguishability of illusory appearances, such as the
bent appearance of a stick partially submerged in water, as compared
with ordinary appearances, this was not the basic argument for sense
data. Their basic argument came from Perceptual Variation and the lack
of a match between the penny as it appears and the penny as they
believed it to be physically.</p>

<p>
A. J. Ayer (1940), contra Price and others, argued that the existence
of sense data is not a factual question but a matter of linguistic
convention. He held that all theorists of perception might agree on
the phenomenal facts about seeing pennies but disagree on the
implications for the real shapes of pennies. One theorist holds that,
as the observer changes position, the penny appears elliptical but
stays round; the theorist endorses sense data (by Illusion). Another
theorist agrees about the series of elliptical appearances with a
moving observer but holds that, when the penny looks elliptical, it
has changed shape to become elliptical (Ayer 1940: 17&ndash;18).
According to Ayer, the competing theories can&rsquo;t be
differentiated empirically (they agree on the elliptical appearances),
and so they become recommendations about perceptual language. Ayer
(1940: ch. 1) found that the Argument from Illusion supports choosing
sense-data language. He developed the language of phenomenalism, in
which statements about material objects are understood as statements
about actual and possible sense data (1940: ch. 5). In his scheme,
even though sense data do not exist unsensed (possible sense data are
merely hypothetical), they need not be classed as mental states (1940:
76&ndash;78). Ayer (1940: 61&ndash;65) departed from the other main
theorists in rejecting an act-object analysis of sensations and their
objects
 [<a href="#cn8">item (8)</a>].</p>
 
<h2 id="MajoCritRespOppo">3. Major Critical Responses or Oppositions</h2>

<p>
The topic of sense data was heavily discussed in the middle decades of
the twentieth century, and some notable criticisms were
 published.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-19" id="ref-19">19</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
Austin&rsquo;s lectures in Oxford (from 1947 on), critical of sense
data, were published in 1962 as <em>Sense and Sensibilia</em>. They
took aim at the doctrine that</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
we never see or otherwise perceive (or &lsquo;sense&rsquo;), or anyhow
we never directly perceive or sense, material objects (or material
things), but only sense-data. (1962: 2)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Austin accuses Ayer (1940), Price (1932), and others of embracing a
&ldquo;scholastic&rdquo; view that analyzes a few poorly understood
words and &ldquo;half-studied &lsquo;facts&rsquo;&rdquo;. By contrast,
he promises to show</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
that our ordinary words are much subtler in their uses, and mark many
more distinctions, than philosophers have realized; and that the facts
of perception, as discovered by, for instance, psychologists but also
as noted by common mortals, are much more diverse and complicated than
has been allowed for. (1962: 3)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Austin&rsquo;s criticisms focus on language use, even while dissenting
from Ayer&rsquo;s view that the issues are entirely terminological
(Austin 1962: ch. 6). He focuses on the &ldquo;argument from
illusion&rdquo; from Ayer (also citing Price), and complains that
ordinary cases of perceptual relativity and variation, such as the
elliptical-looking penny, are not normally taken to be illusions. In
fact, as we have seen, many earlier sense-data theorists would agree.
Austin has little to say about the Argument from Perceptual Variation
from the Classical discussions. He finds the penny example to be
atypical because pennies have stable, sharply bounded shapes, whereas
many objects, such as cats, do not. In considering the Argument from
Illusion, Austin (1962: 52) objects to the continuity premise, which
we have just seen in Price: that if, in one case, we explain a
mismatch between object and perception by introducing a special entity
such as a sense datum, we must then accept that in phenomenally
similar cases we can&rsquo;t have direct, veridical perception. (The
continuity premise remains in dispute today, see Crane &amp; French
2015 [2021].) More generally, sense-data theorists had argued that in
describing elliptical appearances gained from a penny, one adopts a
special sense of &ldquo;see&rdquo; that (a) does not require that an
elliptical material object exists, but (b) does affirm that an
elliptical sense datum exists (1962: ch. 3). Austin&rsquo;s denial
that something elliptical must be seen in such cases draws on ordinary
language. But Price and others distinguished different senses of
perception words advisedly; Austin fails to engage their reasons more
directly. Further, he does not consider theorists who say that we
perceive material things as a result of sensing sense data (e.g.,
Broad 1923: 248).</p>

<p>
Another challenge to sense-data theory sought to reveal a
&ldquo;sense-datum fallacy&rdquo; (Chisholm 1957: 151&ndash;152). The
charge is that sense-data theorists make inferences like this:
something appears elliptical, therefore what we see is an appearance
that is
 elliptical.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-20" id="ref-20">20</a>]</sup>
 The fallacy consists in simply assuming that if something
<em>appears</em> a certain way (such as Price&rsquo;s flat circle
produced by a spherical ball), something must exist that <em>is</em>
that way (a sense datum). Critics then argue that, in asserting that
something appears elliptical (or flat), one might simply be describing
the character of the appearance, not asserting that an item with an
elliptical shape (or flat surface) is present. After all, we normally
do accept that things need not be as they appear. In responding to the
alleged &ldquo;fallacy&rdquo;, it is claimed that sense-data theorists
do not simply move from &ldquo;appears elliptical&rdquo; to &ldquo;is
elliptical&rdquo; (Smith 2002: 35&ndash;36). Rather, the positing of
sense data most commonly arises through acceptance of an act-object
analysis of perception
 [<a href="#cn8">item (8)</a>],
 which also affirms that the objects of perception are directly
apprehended
 [<a href="#cn1">item (1)</a>]
 and that they are as they appear
 [<a href="#cn3">item (3)</a>].
 The arguments from Variation and Illusion may then be used to contend
that we often experience perceptual objects whose properties are
distinct from the properties ascribed to material objects, as in the
penny appearing elliptical, and that from the qualitative similarity
of illusions (or variant appearances) and alleged direct perceptions,
we should conclude that we are always acquainted with sense data. Such
arguments may indeed be challenged, but there has been no simple and
unwary move from <em>appears such</em> to <em>is such</em>.</p>

<p>
Let us consider further the contention that a penny might appear
elliptical in a phenomenally direct way without an elliptical item
being present to the mind. Such views are sometimes known as
&ldquo;appearance theories&rdquo;. One such position is the adverbial
analysis, which holds that phenomenal qualities inhere in the mental
activity of perceiving but without being present as objects or as
property-instances. We perceive the penny itself, but perceive it in a
certain manner: roundly, or elliptically, and redly (Ducasse 1942;
Chisholm 1957: part 3). To say that we are experiencing the penny
elliptically is to say that we enjoy an elliptical appearance without
there being an actually elliptical item present to us; rather, the
appearance is understood simply as our manner of sensing the penny, or
as the way our sensory activity makes us experience the shape of the
penny. A related sort of appearance theory renders the elliptical
appearance as a mental content, or an intentional state, with the
contents elliptical and red (Barnes 1944&ndash;1945). Via intentional
content, one can experience an elliptical spatial structure without
being acquainted with an actually elliptical object. The intentional
content instantiates an appearance as a state of the perceiver, whose
perceptual experience thereby represents the world as being a certain
way, for instance, as containing an elliptical shape. (Intentionalist
theories are found in Dretske 1995 and Tye 1995; an important earlier
formulation is Anscombe 1965.) These two types of theories account for
Variation and Illusion via adverbial or intentional contents that can
also account for veridical cases of perception. Indeed, these theories
can allow that, in some instances (with moderate slant), we see a
penny that is round by seeing it as being round and slanted relative
to the line of sight. Adverbialists would say that we see the penny
roundly; intentionalists, that we see the penny via mental content
that represents it as being round. (On these philosophies of
perception, see Crane &amp; French 2015 [2021].)</p>

<p>
There is a tradition in philosophy and psychology that denies, on
phenomenal grounds, the distinction between impoverished, typically
two-dimensional sensory contents or states and the perception of
objects in three dimensions. One can see William James&rsquo;s
affirmation that sensations are intrinsically world-directed (1890, 2:
1&ndash;3) and exhibit three-dimensional volume (1890, 2:
135&ndash;136) as objecting in advance to the Classical Notion of
sense data. James denies
 <a href="#cn5">item (5)</a>,
 that sense data are neutral about a world beyond them, in favor of a
position that both sensation and perception give us &ldquo;an
immediately present outward reality&rdquo; (1890, 2: 2). The Gestalt
psychologists (Koffka 1935: chs. 1&ndash;3; K&ouml;hler 1929 [1947:
chs. 1&ndash;3]) also adopted a position in which we are perceptually
given a world, not an impoverished content or object distinct from the
world. They made perception of the valence or functional values of the
world into an immediately given aspect of perceptual content. They
also denied that two-dimensional sensations or sense data are the core
from which perceptions are developed: the world is originally given in
 three-dimensions.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-21" id="ref-21">21</a>]</sup>
 The Gestalt theorists drew attention to the phenomena of size and
shape constancy. If two objects of the same size are presented at five
and ten feet, the retinal image of the farther object will be half as
large (in linear height) as the image of the nearby object; yet
observers report them to be phenomenally of the same size (or nearly
so). Similarly, a circle seen at a (moderate) slant projects an
ellipse on the retina but appears circular. These theorists contended
that significant phenomenal differences in size between the two
objects, and the appearance of an elliptical shape, are produced only
under special laboratory conditions or through trained acts of
attention, as might be found in those who are taught to draw (Koffka
1935: 222&ndash;223; K&ouml;hler 1929 [1947: 71&ndash;74]).
Subsequently, the perceptual psychologist James J. Gibson (1950)
agreed with the Gestalt theorists that two-dimensional experiences
that are like sense data arise from acts of attention that
artificially alter our phenomenology away from the three-dimensional
visual world that we normally perceive. Accordingly, the
two-dimensional visual field is not an immediate, primitive object of
perception that is recovered through attention. Rather, it is
constructed through a special effort of attention. Further, in
everyday perception of a circle at a slant (say, your neighbor&rsquo;s
plate at a dinner party), the object looks like a circle at a slant
and so looks circular, not elliptical (1950: chs. 1, 9). In subsequent
writings, Gibson (1966, 1979) developed the notion that sensory
systems are attuned for presenting to perceivers a navigable
environment, a process that does not begin with impoverished
sensations but depends on the sensitivity of the perceptual apparatus
to information about the spatial layout.</p>

<p>
Each of these three theoretical stances denies, on phenomenal grounds,
the two-fold character of perceptual consciousness, that is, the
division into impoverished sensuous element and accompanying
interpretation or object-positing. Rather, sensory experience is
intrinsically world-presenting, spatially expansive (in three
dimensions), and action guiding.</p>

<p>
Finally, Wilfrid Sellars famously sought to unmask the
&ldquo;myth&rdquo; of a pure sensory given to which subjects respond
atomistically in a way that yields an epistemically primitive basis
for knowledge. According to Sellars&rsquo;s highly regarded
&ldquo;Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind&rdquo; (1956), the
&ldquo;classical&rdquo; sense datum position holds that &ldquo;s is
red&rdquo; can be known non-inferentially. Classical sense-data
philosophers</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
have taken givenness to be a fact which presupposes no learning, no
forming of associations, no setting up of stimulus-response
connections. (1956: &sect;6)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
They accept an &ldquo;inconsistent triad&rdquo;:</p>

<div class="indent">

<ol type="A">

<li><em>X senses red sense-content s</em> entails <em>x
non-inferentially knows that s is red</em>.</li>

<li>The ability to sense sense-contents is unacquired.</li>

<li>The ability to know facts of the form <em>x is &phi;</em> is
acquired.</li>
</ol>

<p>
A and B together entail not-C; B and C entail not-A; A and C entail
not-B. (1956: &sect;6, emended)</p>
</div>

<p>
Who allegedly subscribed to this triad? Some of Sellars&rsquo;s
examples draw from early modern philosophy; he effectively attributes
a version of A and B to Locke, Berkeley, and Hume but not, it seems, C
(1956: &sect;28). He criticizes Ayer&rsquo;s linguistic analysis
(1956: &sect;&sect;8&ndash;9) but suggests that it is aimed more at
analyzing object-talk into sense-data talk than at showing sense-data
talk to be epistemically basic (as in A). He briefly alludes to Broad
(1956: &sect;11) and Price (1956: &sect;31), without ascribing the
triad to them. He also devotes considerable attention further on to
the role of an ostensive &ldquo;given&rdquo; within the
&ldquo;positivistic conception of science&rdquo; (1956:
&sect;&sect;43&ndash;44), and it is tempting to surmise that his main
target was a conception of giveness put forward by Carnap,
Reichenbach, and others. In this conception, an account of language
learning and theory building supposes that sense contents, as objects
of ostension, are taken as primitive given elements. To assess that
direction, we would need to interpret the positivist shift from
elementary experiences to protocol sentences as epistemically basic,
which would take us far afield (but see Leitgeb &amp; Carus 2020).</p>

<p>
A prominent reading of Locke indeed has him impute epistemic authority
to sensory ideas as giving knowledge of their causes (Ayers 1991: vol.
1, ch. 18). Locke is attributed A and B but not C. It is doubtful that
Descartes, who distinguished sensing from judging, would endorse A.
Among the classical sense-data theorists, many would deny A,
distinguishing sensing and &ldquo;acquaintance&rdquo; from
&ldquo;knowing that&rdquo;; sensing red may usually and
non-inferentially lead to knowing that s is red, but not as a simple
matter of entailment. Russell explicitly denied A; sensing may be
unacquired but &ldquo;knowing that&rdquo; involves judging, not mere
sensing, and the universals that enter judgments of sensory quality
(such as whiteness) are acquired, affirming C. Sellars himself rejects
A, complicates B, and affirms C, adopting (by assumption) a position
of &ldquo;psychological nominalism&rdquo; (1956: &sect;29), according
to which kind-terms, including color terms, and the ability to be
aware of color instances, are learned as part of a linguistic and
conceptual web of meaning (holism).</p>

<p>
In his holism about color terms, Sellars raised an objection that
would engage Russell&rsquo;s conception of atomic statements about
sense data as epistemically basic (Russell&rsquo;s psychologically and
logically primitive data). Sellars (1956) constructs an elaborate myth
of his own, in which our behaviorist ancestors must learn to treat
thoughts as internal states of one another and subsequently to
self-report their own thoughts (1956: &sect;&sect;47&ndash;59). He
extends the story from the attribution of inner thoughts to one
another to the positing of internal sensory impressions (1956:
&sect;&sect;60&ndash;62). His view differs from Russell&rsquo;s
position in making the ability to be aware of red sense experiences
depend on the acquisition of a sophisticated web of meaning and
theory. Sellars also departs from Russell in denying that the
universal <em>red</em>, which is needed for knowing that s is red, is
&ldquo;easily acquired&rdquo; (1912 [1997: 101]). There are no easily
acquired atomic color predicates to describe the sense data that
ground knowledge. Sellars asserts that</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
instead of coming to have a concept of something because we have
noticed that sort of thing, to have the ability to notice a sort of
thing is already to have the concept of that sort of thing, and cannot
account for it. (1956: &sect;45)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
He claims the advantage that, through this social process of
acquisition, sense impressions lose &ldquo;absolute privacy&rdquo; and
become objects of intersubjective knowledge (1956: &sect;62), and here
he directly departs from
 <a href="#cn7">item (7)</a>
 (privacy) in the Classical Notion. But he has also rejected item (5),
that &ldquo;Our awareness of such properties of sense data does not
involve the affirmation or conception of any object beyond the
datum&rdquo;, and presumably item (8) as well, &ldquo;Sense data are
distinct from the act of sensing, or the act by which we are aware of
them&rdquo;. Sellars concludes his elaborate myth with the result that
sense data are not a pre-existing core of sensory content, but are
rather the product of experience and theory (or cognitive development,
including acquired concepts). Accordingly, he rejects the view that
sense data are immediately present to the mind prior to learning and
are present by way of a simple act of direct acquaintance that is
distinct from the datum itself. Sellars&rsquo;s holistic conception
that a web of concepts must be formed prior to the acquisition of the
ability to observe and self-report the presence of sense particulars
may offer better reasons for rejecting items (5) and (8) of the Classical
Notion than are found through the inconsistent triad.</p>

<h2 id="SensDataRece">4. Sense Data Recently</h2>

<p>
In philosophies of mind and perception, sense data came under
increasing attack in the 1950s and 1960s and fell out of favor. But
there have been attempts to restore some version of the theory. Often,
but not always, these efforts sought to sustain mental sense data as
the immediate objects of perception. The most extensive efforts are by
Frank Jackson, in <em>Perception</em> (1977), and Howard Robinson, in
<em>Perception</em> (1994) and <em>Perception and Idealism</em>
(2022).</p>

<p>
Jackson set out to support the conclusion that the immediate objects
of perception are mental and that they represent physical objects. He
first argues that what we immediately perceive are sense data, which
are colored patches. His argument appeals to the fact that, when we
perceive an object, we are not in immediate contact with the whole
object but only a portion of it, and that we are immediately aware of
the color and shape of this portion. We see the object in virtue of
seeing its surface, and the basic form of seeing its surface is to see
its color and shape via sense data that represent that surface. The
&ldquo;in virtue of&rdquo; relation does all the work here; Jackson
does not draw on, and rejects, the arguments from Variation and
Illusion (1977: chs. 1, 4, 7).</p>

<p>
This conclusion does not say whether sense data are mental or physical
(1977: 119). The argument that sense data are mental proceeds by
seeking to establish that colors are mental items. The argument is
indirect, from the conclusion that colors are not physical. This
conclusion, in turn, relies on the claim that color is not a
&ldquo;scientific property&rdquo;, that only scientific properties
exist in the external world and affect the eyes and brain, and that
color then can only be a mental entity caused by neural activity
(1977: ch. 5).</p>

<p>
The argument relies on a conception of scientific or physical
properties that is widespread in philosophy: that physics has no place
for phenomenal properties or perceptual experience. Physics posits
particles and forces, which may cause perceptual experience in a
suitable perceiver, but which themselves are colorless. Jackson is
certainly right that physics today does not accept something akin to
phenomenal color as a fundamental physical property; rather, it
accepts wavelengths and brain processes, and these possess no such
fundamental property. Prior to the evolution of color-sentient beings,
there presumably were no phenomenal experiences of color, hence none
to be counted among physical phenomena.</p>

<p>
Nonetheless, physics, especially in the science of optics, accepts the
&ldquo;subjective <em>perception</em> of color&rdquo; (Nelson 2017:
125) as an object of
 study,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-22" id="ref-22">22</a>]</sup>
 just as acoustics accepts the experience of pitch as a physical
phenomenon (Weld &amp; Palmer 1925: 322). Arguably, phenomenal
experiences need not be reduced or even reducible to basic physical
processes in order to be counted among physical phenomena and hence
among items to be investigated in the hard sciences. Accordingly,
color experience needn&rsquo;t be decisively categorized as
&ldquo;non-physical&rdquo;, as Jackson claims. Its ultimate ontology
is left open. It might be a mental content as in an intentional theory
or other appearance
 theory.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-23" id="ref-23">23</a>]</sup>
 The color property might be analyzed relationally, as a surface
property with the power to produce a color experience, which
needn&rsquo;t be a colored sense datum. The metaphysical status of
phenomenal experience need not be decided in order for the conditions
of such experience to be examined in the science of physics.</p>

<p>
Robinson (1994, 2022) sets forth the most extensive case for sense
data in recent times. His first book starts from a history of
sense-data theory, which he sees as deriving from Locke, Berkeley, and
Hume. Robinson thus disagrees with the history offered above as
regards Berkeley and Locke. In both books, he specifies a standard
conception of sense data: that such data possess the sensible
qualities they appear to have, and that they are not intrinsically
representational (they do not intrinsically refer beyond themselves;
they have no intentional content). He takes the Argument from Illusion
to be the primary classical argument for sense data in the twentieth
century (though not much invoked before). He finds that illusion and
hallucination are most plausibly understood as showing that
phenomenally and metaphysically indistinguishable contents are
manifest by them. Most of the other arguments he examines in the first
book are related to Variation or to Illusion and Hallucination, or to
Jackson&rsquo;s argument from science about color. In the second book,
he strengthens his arguments on several points. He elaborates upon the
argument from Illusion and Hallucination as vanquishing na&iuml;ve
realism and direct &ldquo;relational&rdquo; theories (2022: ch.
1&ndash;2). And he expands his arguments against intentional or
representational theories, relying heavily on phenomenological
considerations (2022: ch. 3&ndash;5, 7).</p>

<p>
Some of Robinson&rsquo;s arguments are new to our discussion, such as
the Time-Lag Argument. It notes that the light reaching our eyes from
stars may be thousands of years old and so the star may have ceased to
exist. On the assumption that the object of veridical perception
exists, our perception cannot be of the actual star, and hence must be
of another object. But all perception involves a causal process that
unfolds in time; hence, on the assumption that perception must be
simultaneous with its object, we never perceive the actual properties
of the things that reflect or emit light (1994: ch. 3). This argument
can be extended to a Causal Argument (1994: ch. 6) which maintains
that we can be made to seem to perceive objects by non-standard causal
processes, such as those that underlie hallucination. In such cases,
we don&rsquo;t perceive an external object and so there must be
another object that we perceive, having the properties that appear to
us (invoking
 <a href="#cn3">item (3) of the Classical Notion</a>).
 These cases are taken to be phenomenally indistinguishable from
normal perception. Hence, arguing that phenomenally indistinguishable
items should be classified as the same type of object (as in
Price&rsquo;s Argument from Illusion, Sec. 2.2.3, above), we should
conclude that in the normal case we also don&rsquo;t directly perceive
an external object.</p>

<p>
Robinson further contends that, if his arguments for sense data are
sound, this finding would support a phenomenalistic idealism over
physical realism (1994: chs. 8&ndash;9; 2022, pt. 2).</p>

<p>
For those who would avoid both mental and non-mental perceptual
intermediaries, the theory of &ldquo;Naturalized Sense Data&rdquo;
(NSD) is of interest (Berm&uacute;dez 2000). It distinguishes between
what is directly perceived and what is immediately perceived. In this
scheme, three-dimensional objects are directly perceived but not
immediately perceived. We immediately perceive the portion of the
surface of the object that is visible at any moment. Because we
immediately perceive a portion of the three-dimensional object, we are
able to make ostensive reference to the three-dimensional object
itself and so, it is claimed, to perceive it directly. NSD assumes
that na&iuml;ve realism holds that we immediately perceive
three-dimensional objects as a whole; NSD therefore rejects na&iuml;ve
realism, on the grounds that we immediately perceive only
surface-portions (2000: 369&ndash;372). NSD objects to traditional
sense-data theory as placing an unnecessary intermediary between the
perceiver and ostended objects (2000: 370&ndash;371).</p>

<p>
There are two problems with this position. First, as Berm&uacute;dez
observes, if it is to avoid bringing in mentalistic factors, it
requires a physicalist account of color as a surface property, which
has not been forthcoming (2000: 368, n. 14; 373). Second, if a
physical surface-portion provides the immediate object of perception,
then it provides the phenomenal content of our perception. When Moore
endorsed surface-portions as immediate objects, he noted the problem
that if a surface of a round penny (seen obliquely) is the object of
perception, it should appear round. Since he held that it did not, he
felt compelled to say that the sense datum appears elliptical but is
round, thereby sundering the immediacy by bringing a separate
&ldquo;appearance&rdquo; into the mix. A defender of NSD might counter
Moore&rsquo;s response by saying that the penny in fact looks round
and slanted, so there is no disparity between physical surface-portion
and phenomenal perception. But then Price&rsquo;s objection arises.
Price allowed for what is now called full shape constancy within a few
feet of the observer: the round penny at a slant would appear round.
But he noted that, beyond a few feet, constancy starts to break
 down.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-24" id="ref-24">24</a>]</sup>
 This creates a problem for NSD: to account for the phenomenal
diminution, it would need to recognize &ldquo;appearances&rdquo; with
content that differs from the actual structure of a surface-portion.
This violates the intent of NSD, which is to establish an account in
which the phenomenal content of perceptual experience is provided by
the properties of the surface-portion itself.</p>

<h2 id="Conc">5. Conclusion</h2>

<p>
The core notion of a sense datum is an immediately given minimal
perceptual object, consisting in vision of a shaped patch of color.
Often, but not always, the visual datum was considered to be two
dimensional. It typically was contrasted with the perception of a
three-dimensional material object of a particular kind and displaying
a panoply of further properties. In the classical period (the first
several decades of the twentieth century), sense data were typically
taken to be non-mental and mind-independent, and were sometimes
regarded as representing material objects. In some cases, sense data
or their kin were taken to be elemental constituents in the
composition or bundling of sense particulars. These particulars did
not refer to material objects per se but, when conjoined, composed a
perceptual world (as in Russell 1914a and 1914b). More recently, sense
data have been viewed as mental items (e.g., Jackson 1977), and
perhaps as leading to idealism (Robinson 1994, 2022). In a few cases,
sense data have been equated with portions of the surface of a
material object (Moore 1918&ndash;19: 23&ndash;24; Berm&uacute;dez
2000). Across the history of sense-data theory, the data have been
variously considered to be ontologically neutral (Moore 1913&ndash;14;
Price 1932: 18&ndash;20); or to be mind-independent third things,
neither mental nor physical (Russell 1912: ch. 1); to be
instrinsically representational, or not (Moore 1925, 1957); to be
mental objects (Jackson, Robinson); or to be portions of the surface
of physical objects (Moore, Berm&uacute;dez).</p>

<p>
The theory of sense data is a species of a genera of positions in
perception theory, which make a distinction between an impoverished
sense impression and the perception, affirmation, or judgment of a
further entity, usually a material object. Although sharing this
distinction, other species in this genus differ from sense-data theory
in various ways, the most fundamental being that they do not posit an
object having the properties of color and shape as found in conscious
perception
 [<a href="#cn3">item (3)</a>].
 Some views render perceived properties as appearances. One type of
appearance theory holds that shape, color, and other perceived
object-properties are intentional contents. Another type holds that
experiences of shapes and colors are manners or modes of perceiving
(adverbialism). Adherents of both types of theory reject the
act-object analysis and so would reject item (8) of the Classical
Notion; and, of course, they would also reject items (1) and (2). In
another vein, some versions of na&iuml;ve realism hold that we
immediately perceive the surface of an object from a point of view and
in three dimensions, and that this perspectival perception of a
surface provides perceptual content; but these contents are said not
to involve sense-data because they are not themselves mental (No&euml;
2004, Brewer 2011). At present, these various positions enjoy more
favor than do theories that posit sense data as mental objects or as
third things. Indeed, recent discussions in the philosophy of
perception frequently use sense data as an example of a concept or an
entity to be avoided (e.g., Fish 2010: ch. 2).</p>

<p>
Theories that distinguish impoverished sense perception from object
perception and cognition have been challenged by what Firth
(1949&ndash;50) called the &ldquo;percept theory&rdquo;, according to
which immediate visual experience is of a three-dimensional visual
world that manifests object-properties and possibilities for action.
This is the tradition of James, the Gestalt psychologists, Gibson, and
some of the appearance theories (e.g., Smith 2002). Such theories have
grown in favor recently. But sense-data theories have also shown
development. Many sense-data theories recognize the immediate objects
of perception to be three-dimensional. This would permit a penny seen
at a moderate slant to be described as appearing as a circle at a
slant, not an ellipse. Finally, although treatment of phenomenal color
as a primitive property the instances of which literally inhere in
sense data (whether such data are considered to be surface portions,
mental objects, or third things) is not widely favored, it is also
true that, metaphysically, there is no settled home for phenomenally
experienced color. The endeavor to account for the phenomenal
characteristics of objects and their properties is ongoing.</p>
